      },
      "config": {
        "path": "/Users/alec/Documents/Math/research_website_catalogue_20261024/papers/23202966/reviews/editorial/EDITORIAL_CONFIG.json",
        "bytes": 12548,
        "SHA256": "ee1590b98cfd8d21a7bc5fc388541729e568afcf0fb6be52b122c79f8f2dd50f"
      },
      "Bib": {
        "path": "/Users/alec/Documents/Math/research_website_catalogue_20261024/papers/23202966/reviews/editorial/candidate/citation.bib",
        "bytes": 325,
        "SHA256": "c2da3566b5348ab904a5ea82ddbb177f8f14591ae9b9de10203dfdf0cd384b41"
      },
      "abstract_full": "We give explicit fixed-binary-alphabet consequences of OpenAI’s two relation-liveness lower bounds. Encoding each relation on h points by its h² adjacency bits gives a language recognized by a one-way nondeterministic automaton with Nₕ=(3h³−h)/2+2 states. Every equivalent two-way deterministic automaton, and every two-way nondeterministic automaton for its full binary complement, requires 2^{Ω(h)}=2^{Ω(Nₕ^(1/3))} states. We specify all transition rules, including an exact sh²-state pullback for an s-state binary target. The binary language rejects incomplete blocks; a fooling set proves that its one-way nondeterministic state complexity is Θ(h³). The exponential obstructions are inherited from the two upstream theorems; binary adjacency coding is established machinery.",
      "title": "Binary consequences of relation-liveness automata lower bounds",
